#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# elliptic_log

#% text
# `elliptic_log([x, y], [a, b])` gives the elliptic logarithm of the point `(x, y)` of the curve `y^2 = x^3 + a x^2 + b x`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The value is the integral of `dt/(2 y)` from infinity to the point: with `x = X - a/3` the curve is the Weierstrass curve of `g2 = 4 a^2/3 - 4 b`, `g3 = 4 a b/3 - 8 a^3/27`, and the value is the `u` with `weierstrass_p(u) = x + a/3` and `weierstrass_p_prime(u) = 2 y` (`inverse_weierstrass_p`).

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values; closed forms among numbers are evaluated at their precision.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
elliptic_log([2, 5], [3, 2.5])
#% output
#= -0.581447294993539

#% cell
n(elliptic_log([1, 3], [2, 6]), 25)
#% output
#= -0.70985188008011573259779996833

#% cell
x = 0.3; elliptic_log([x, sqrt(x^3 - 5*x^2 + x)], [-5, 1])
#% output
#= -0.7256311852272992 + 1.0681781783521944im

#% cell
elliptic_log([2 + im, -im*sqrt(15)], [1 - im, -12.0])
#% output
#= 0.6317190201568895 - 0.25018133814227805im

#% cell
n(elliptic_log([0, 0], [0, 1]))
#% output
#= 1.8540746773013719

